(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-benign-skin-lesions-quiz"]={"title":"Benign Skin Lesions Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does nevus flammeus look like early in life, and later?","choices":["Early, a bright red papule or plaque that grows rapidly; later, a pale involuted patch with residual telangiectasia","Early, small pink macules on the nape of the neck; later, complete fading within the first year","Early, a smooth firm deep red papule under five millimetres; later, more of the same on the trunk","Early, a flat blanchable patch; later, a raised thickened plaque of deeper colour"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — early it is well-circumscribed and macular, pink to red to purple, usually unilateral with fairly sharp midline cutoffs, and it darkens with crying, fever or overheating. Later the vasculature dilates and it may become a raised, thickened, deep-red to purple plaque. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is Crowe's sign, and how does it differ from a café au lait spot?","choices":["A wave-like fluid shift on palpation of a fluctuant sacral swelling","Intertriginous freckling; the freckles are under five millimetres","Axillary freckling over fifteen millimetres, larger than a café au lait spot, present from birth","A dimple appearing on lateral compression of a firm dermal nodule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — axillary and inguinal, smaller than café au lait spots, grouped together, and more prominent with sun exposure. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What does debridement remove, and why?","choices":["The entire cyst capsule, because leaving any part of it behind essentially guarantees that the lesion recurs","The cone-shaped central keratin core, because it is what transmits the pressure down into the skin","The proliferating endothelial cells, because they are what drive the lesion to keep enlarging over months","Necrotic tissue, eschar and slough, because they promote infection, delay granulation and impede healing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all three reasons are given together. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which referrals are named in the management of a pressure injury?","choices":["Cardiology, after screening for hyperlipidemia","A wound care specialist, and surgery for debridement or wound closure","Podiatry, and dermatology for a deep shave biopsy of the base","A vascular anomalies specialist, and paediatrics for growth monitoring"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — management otherwise depends on the stage. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What produces a clavus, and what does the pressure create?","choices":["Fibroblast proliferation within the dermis, causing a firm nodule with a hyperpigmented halo and a positive dimple sign","Mechanical trauma such as ill-fitting shoes, causing hyperkeratosis with a cone-shaped central core of hard keratin pointing into the skin","Broad-area friction such as manual work, causing diffuse hyperkeratosis with no central core and a poorly defined margin","Human papillomavirus infection of the epidermis, causing a cauliflower-like surface with a blackened centre and interrupted skin lines"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the central core is what separates a corn from a callus. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What should a patient with a pyogenic granuloma be told?","choices":["The lesion is premalignant and must be excised urgently to prevent transformation","The lesion is infectious and household contacts should be examined","The lesion will fade within a year and no follow-up is necessary","It is benign and often resolves over months to years; treatment is available if wanted"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — treatments exist for cosmetic reasons or to prevent bleeding. If it recurs, early follow-up is appropriate, because small lesions are easier to treat than large ones. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"When may radiation be used for a keloid?","choices":["As a first-line treatment before any surgery is attempted","Only once intralesional steroid and compression have both failed over a year","Only in patients who cannot tolerate silicone sheeting or compression garments","During the first two weeks after the keloid has been excised"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is an adjunct to surgery rather than a primary treatment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How soon after the injury does each of these scars appear, and what happens with time?","choices":["Both develop months after the trauma and neither improves without treatment","Hypertrophic scars develop soon after surgery and usually improve with time; keloids may develop months after the trauma and rarely improve","Hypertrophic scars develop months after the trauma and rarely improve; keloids develop soon after surgery and usually improve","Both develop within four weeks of the injury and both flatten over the following year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — timing and trajectory both separate them. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What causes a spider angioma, and which states are named?","choices":["Proliferation of endothelial cells from a gene mutation, present in the first weeks of life","Dilation of dermal capillaries present from birth, darkening and thickening throughout life","Dilation of preexisting vessels in estrogen excess; no proliferation","Proliferation of new capillaries and venules with age, with no hormonal association at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the named states are pregnancy or oral contraceptive use, cirrhosis and liver failure. Those from pregnancy or the pill resolve after delivery or after stopping the pill. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Where is nevus simplex most common, and what makes it more noticeable?","choices":["Head and neck; crying","Trunk; exposure to sunlight","Extremities; cold temperature","Face and upper trunk; pregnancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — pink to erythematous blanchable macules or patches, single or multiple. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What defines a deep tissue pressure injury?","choices":["Persistent non-blanchable deep red or purple discolouration; the skin can be intact or non-intact","Obscured full thickness loss whose extent cannot be determined because of slough or eschar","A localised area of non-blanchable erythema of intact skin, without discolouration in depth","Full thickness skin and tissue loss exposing fascia, muscle, tendon, ligament, cartilage or bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the colour is the finding, and intact skin does not exclude it. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How does an acute pilonidal abscess present, and how does chronic disease present?","choices":["Acutely, sudden pain and swelling in or along the gluteal cleft; chronically, recurrent drainage and pain from one or more sinus tracts","Acutely, recurrent purulent drainage from several sinus tracts; chronically, sudden pain and swelling over the sacrum","Acutely, a rapidly growing vascular nodule that bleeds easily; chronically, a flat uniformly pigmented patch","Acutely, non-blanchable erythema over intact skin; chronically, a deep ulcer extending as far as bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a hair may occasionally be seen protruding from a sinus opening. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What does pulsed dye laser do to a hypertrophic scar?","choices":["Reduces pruritus by destroying cutaneous nerve endings","Reduces recurrence by removing the entire cyst capsule","Reduces scar erythema by reducing neovascularization","Reduces scar height by cross-linking collagen fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it also decreases scar height and improves texture. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the only reliable way to diagnose a keratoacanthoma?","choices":["Clinical appearance alone","Response to a trial of cryotherapy","Biopsy","Dermoscopy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the clinical differential includes three malignancies. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What opportunity does the deck say a new lesion in a sun-exposed area creates?","choices":["To advise avoiding cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing","To counsel on sunscreen, peak-hour sun avoidance and skin checks","To screen for hyperlipidemia and assess cardiac risk","To refer to podiatry if the patient is diabetic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the general education point the deck attaches to every benign lesion in a sun-exposed area. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"How does a callus differ from a corn?","choices":["It comes from repeated scratching, is lichenified with exaggerated skin markings, is plaque-like, and itches intensely","It comes from broad-area pressure, is diffusely thickened without a central core, is larger and irregular, and is usually painless","It comes from focal pressure, has a cone-shaped keratin core, is smaller and well defined, and hurts on direct pressure","It comes from viral infection, has a blackened centre, interrupts the skin lines, and hurts on side pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — no core, bigger, vaguer, and it does not hurt. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does a lipoma feel like, and when does it cause symptoms?","choices":["Firm nodules with a hyperpigmented halo, about half to one centimetre; often the most painful skin tumour","Firm bulbous nodules or elevated plaques extending beyond a wound; pruritic or burning if symptomatic","Soft pedunculated papules on a narrow stalk, one to ten millimetres; symptomatic only if torn","Soft painless subcutaneous nodules, rubbery, usually under five centimetres"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — asymptomatic unless adjoining structures are invaded, and it can occur anywhere on the body. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"What are the first-line treatments for an infantile hemangioma?","choices":["Intralesional corticosteroid alone, with fluorouracil added if the lesion continues to enlarge","Surgical excision in every case, performed before the proliferative phase has finished","Cryotherapy with liquid nitrogen, repeated until the lesion has flattened completely","Beta-blockers and corticosteroids; both are named first line"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — propranolol by mouth or timolol topically, whose mechanism is not well understood, and corticosteroids given topically, intralesionally or orally to slow growth and shrink the lesion. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which three features separate a wart from a corn or callus?","choices":["It interrupts the skin lines, has a blackened centre, and is not confined to pressure areas","It preserves the skin lines, has a pale centre, and occurs only over pressure areas","It interrupts the skin lines, has a pale centre, and occurs only on the plantar surface","It preserves the skin lines, has a blackened centre, and is not confined to pressure areas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — corns and calluses both let the skin lines run through. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"In whom and where do skin tags occur, and how common are they?","choices":["Increased in females and obese patients, in areas of friction such as the neck, axilla and groin; present in sixty per cent of people by age seventy","Increased in males two to one, on the face, the scalp, the neck and the trunk; very common and occurring at any adult age","Increased in females two to one, on the legs and the arms, and found in all races; a common lesion appearing after minor trauma","Increased in males three to one, over the coccyx and along the gluteal cleft; recurrence after treatment is common"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — very common, and increasingly so with age. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What patient education is given for pilonidal disease?","choices":["Never cut or pull the lesion off at home, as it will bleed","Use sunscreen and perform periodic skin self-examination","Maintain good hygiene, and seek care if an abscess occurs","Avoid stretching the scar and avoid hot baths after surgery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — recurrence is common, so this is ongoing advice. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the differential diagnosis for a lipoma?","choices":["Epidermal cyst, dermatofibroma and abscess","Squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma and amelanotic melanoma","Milia, xanthelasma and basal cell carcinoma","Nevus flammeus and pyogenic granuloma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is typically clinical. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"What are the four phases of wound healing, in order?","choices":["Proliferation, hemostasis, inflammation, remodeling","Hemostasis, proliferation, inflammation, remodeling","Hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, remodeling","Inflammation, hemostasis, remodeling, proliferation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — abnormal healing is loss of control over this balance. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"When is Mohs surgery indicated for a keratoacanthoma?","choices":["Only after intralesional methotrexate has failed to reduce the size of the lesion","Only in immunosuppressed patients, in whom the lesion behaves more aggressively","For large or recurrent lesions, or lesions in areas with cosmetic or functional considerations","For every lesion, because the recurrence rate after simple excision approaches one hundred per cent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — otherwise standard elliptical excision is used. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What does the deck call the best measure against pressure injury, and what does it include?","choices":["Occlusive dressings: silicone gel sheeting worn twelve to twenty-four hours a day over every bony prominence at risk","Prevention: frequent skin assessment, nutrition assessment, moisture control and skin care, repositioning every two hours, pain management, improved mobility and specialty mattresses","Early debridement: removal of the necrotic tissue, eschar and slough that promote infection, delay granulation and impede healing, as soon as any injury is identified at any stage","Prophylactic antibiotics: routinely covering skin flora in every immobile patient in order to prevent any wound infection before it has a chance to start"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — barrier creams and incontinence management sit under moisture control. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What laboratory testing does an epidermoid cyst require?","choices":["Fasting lipid panel to screen for a lipid disorder","Punch biopsy of the cyst wall before any excision","Usually none","Culture of the expressed material in every case"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is clinical. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which risk factors are listed for keratoacanthoma?","choices":["Obesity, repeated local trauma or irritation, a sedentary lifestyle, increased hair density in the natal cleft, and a positive family history for the same lesion in a first-degree relative","African American, Hispanic or Asian ancestry, together with any surgical incision, traumatic wound, burn, vaccination site, case of chickenpox, acne or even a minor scratch","Preterm birth, female sex, Caucasian ancestry, low birth weight, a multiple gestation birth, and a placental abnormality in the pregnancy","Age over forty, sun exposure, very fair skin that always burns and never tans, male sex, tattoos with red ink, skin trauma such as lasers, surgery or cryotherapy, and human papillomavirus infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the red ink and the iatrogenic trauma are the unexpected ones. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Where is a keratoacanthoma believed to arise from, and what is its relationship to squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["The pilosebaceous unit; it is argued to be a variant of invasive squamous cell carcinoma","The eccrine sweat duct; it is histopathologically quite distinct from squamous cell carcinoma and carries no malignant potential at all","The dermal fibroblast; it is histopathologically similar to a hypertrophic scar and does not ever behave in a malignant fashion","The sebaceous gland; it is a crowding of sebocytes within an enlarged gland, with no known potential for malignant transformation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is histopathologically similar to squamous cell carcinoma, and strong arguments support classifying it as a variant of the invasive form. That is why the standard of care is to excise or destroy it rather than watch it. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the differential diagnosis given for a keloid?","choices":["Hypertrophic scar, dermatofibroma, foreign-body granuloma","Squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma, amelanotic melanoma","Cystic acne, lipoma, neurofibroma, basal cell carcinoma","Cherry angioma, malignant melanoma, squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the same three are listed for hypertrophic scar. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is a syringoma, and when does it appear?","choices":["A benign enlargement of sebaceous glands, appearing with age and more often with immunosuppression","A benign neoplasm of eccrine ducts, appearing at puberty and more often in females","A benign neoplasm of endothelial cells, appearing in the first weeks of life and more often in females","A benign nerve sheath tumour, appearing at puberty and increasing with age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — eccrine, meaning sweat gland. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 58"}]};
